[178.756861, "o", "\u001b[34m##\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[34mHow the polyglot works\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37mThe\u001b[0m"]
[178.79814, "o", "\u001b[37m file is split into two regions\u001b[0m"]
[178.804377, "o", "\u001b[37m, each hidden from\u001b[0m"]
[178.839408, "o", "\u001b[37m the \u001b[0m"]
[178.891245, "o", "\u001b[35m*\u001b[0m\u001b[35mother\u001b[0m\u001b[35m*\u001b[0m\u001b[37m language:\u001b[0m"]
[178.931893, "o", "\r\n\r\n"]
[179.025615, "o", "\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33mLines 1–3 — hide the C code from Python\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m"]
[179.052679, "o", "\r\n"]
[179.053172, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m"]
[179.081478, "o", "\r\n"]
[179.081644, "o", "\u001b[34m#\u001b[0m\u001b[34mif\u001b[0m"]
[179.083148, "o", "\u001b[37m 0\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[179.083215, "o", "\u001b[37m\"\"\"\"\u001b[0m"]
[179.091359, "o", "\r\n\u001b[34m#\u001b[0m\u001b[34mendif\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[179.827126, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- *Python*: `#if 0` is a comment, then `\"\"\"\"` opens a triple-quoted string (contents start with the 4th `\"`).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- *C*: `#if 0` skips to `#endif`, so the quotes are never seen as tokens.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m**Line 49 — close the Python string while staying invisible to C**\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[179.827187, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m/\u001b[0m"]
[179.829642, "o", "\u001b[35m*\u001b[0m\u001b[35m\"\"\"#\u001b[0m\u001b[35m*\u001b[0m\u001b[37m/\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[182.374796, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- *Python*: it's still inside the string, so `/*` is just content; the `\"\"\"` terminates the string; `#*/` is then a comment.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- *C*: it's one complete `/* ... */` comment — the quotes vanish in translation phase 3, so the preprocessor never sees an odd quote.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m(Using `\"\"\"` here instead of `/*\"\"\"#*/` produces the harmless but ugly *\"missing terminating `\"` character\"* warning; this form avoids it entirely. The 4-quote opener is for the same reason — an odd quote count inside the skipped block triggers that warning.)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m**Lines 50–70 — hide the Python code from C** with a plain `#if 0 … #endif` (both lines are Python comments).\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m## Implementation notes\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m- **Python**: iterative `f(0)=0, f(1)=1` loop with arbitrary-precision ints.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m- **C**: no 64-bit ceiling — a base-10⁹ bignum (`unsigned int` limbs, 20000 limbs ≈ 180k digits) so the C output matches Python's exactly for any input, not just `n ≤ 93`. Limb arrays are rotated by pointer, no copying.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m## Verification\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[182.749414, "o", "\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m$ python3 --version        -> Python 3.12.3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m$ gcc --version            -> gcc (Ubuntu 13.3.0-...) 13.3.0\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m-\u001b[0m"]
